      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Fat bloom in dark chocolate occurs when water molecules migrate to the chocolate's surface, dissolving cocoa butter, and then moving back due to temperature changes, causing fat to crystallize on the surface. To prevent it, focus on creating the right crystallization by maintaining the correct tempering temperatures, ideally around 31-32°C for dark chocolate. Ensure your chocolate cools evenly without being too cold, store at a consistent temperature, and avoid exposing the chocolate to hot water after tempering.",
